{"nodes": [{"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_dispatcher_tuning", "label": "Dispatcher tuning (page)", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_microtaskdispatcher", "label": "MicrotaskDispatcher", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Microtasks bypass the event loop for roughly 50x faster scheduling, but a CPU-tight actor on microtask starves I/O \u2014 use it only where the actor shares no system with HTTP traffic and awaits no I/O itself."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_immediatedispatcher", "label": "ImmediateDispatcher (default)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Yields between each message so I/O callbacks interleave naturally: good HTTP latency at slightly higher per-message overhead, and right for most apps."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_throughputdispatcher", "label": "ThroughputDispatcher", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "throughput is queued turns drained per tick across every actor sharing the dispatcher, not messages and not per actor. It earns its keep only for a group: given to one actor it drains a queue that never holds more than that actor single pending turn, so its batch is always exactly 1 (#409)."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_per_actor_throughput", "label": "Per-actor throughput budget", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The setting that removes scheduling overhead for a busy actor \u2014 worth roughly 2-3x on a tell-driven workload \u2014 and its trade-off is direct: budget x handler time is the delay every timer, socket read and other actor can see. A batch always ends early on an empty mailbox, a stop or suspend, or an exhausted throttle bucket, so the budget is a ceiling, never a commitment."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_scheduling_round_trip_cost", "label": "What a message costs when nothing is watching", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The scheduling round trip dominates the message path at about 2.4 us against tens of nanoseconds for a raw mailbox operation, which is why a faster queue or a leaner handler moves the number far less than it feels like it should. The per-message instrumentation overhead with metrics and tracing off was removed in #411; handles are now resolved once per message."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_per_actor_dispatcher", "label": "Per-actor dispatcher", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Mix freely \u2014 a compute-only actor on microtasks while HTTP handlers stay on the default. What it is not good for is batching."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_system_wide_dispatcher", "label": "System-wide dispatcher", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Overrides the default for every actor that does not specify otherwise; useful for batch-only systems with no HTTP traffic."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_measuring_wait_vs_handler", "label": "Measuring: wait versus handler", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Wait p99 high with handler p99 low means dispatcher tuning helps; handler p99 high means no dispatcher setting makes it faster and raising the budget makes latency worse for everything sharing the runtime. Queueing delay used to be inferred from the p50-p99 spread, which conflated it with variance in the work itself."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_dispatcher_queue_delay_reading", "label": "Is the dispatcher itself the queue?", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Delay p99 high means turns are competing \u2014 split the dispatcher or lower its throughput; delay p99 low with mailbox wait p99 high means the turns start promptly and one actor own queue is the backlog, so raise that actor withThroughput instead of touching the dispatcher."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_heuristics", "label": "Dispatcher heuristics", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "HTTP plus actors takes the default; compute-heavy without HTTP takes microtask; batch processing with many actors takes ThroughputDispatcher; a single bottleneck actor takes the default plus withThroughput."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_tuning_before_measuring", "label": "Tuning before measuring", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The default works for most apps; tune only when metrics show latency or throughput problems the dispatcher could plausibly cause, and stay below about 1000 turns per tick since 100K without yielding starves everything else in the process."}], "edges": [{"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_dispatcher_tuning", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_microtaskdispatcher", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_dispatcher_tuning", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_immediatedispatcher", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_dispatcher_tuning", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_throughputdispatcher", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_dispatcher_tuning", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_heuristics", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_per_actor_throughput", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_throughputdispatcher", "relation": "rationale_for", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_per_actor_throughput", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_immediatedispatcher", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_scheduling_round_trip_cost", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_per_actor_throughput", "relation": "rationale_for", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_per_actor_dispatcher", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_tuning_dispatcher_tuning_microtaskdispatcher", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/tuning/dispatcher-tuning.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_sr